curiosity. As she approached the stone, the forest fell silent. Every bird held its song, every leaf its rustle. Time seemed to hold its breath. The woman reached out and placed her hand upon the stone. In that instant, a ripple surged through the ground, like a heartbeat awakened. The carvings glowed brighter, and the symbols shifted subtly under her touch. She whispered something ancient, something remembered from a dream or perhaps another life. And the forest listened. Trees bent slightly toward her, the wind wrapped around her like an embrace. What she saw in that moment would have broken the minds of lesser souls, for it was not just a vision but an understanding—a truth too vast to contain within words. She saw the threads of fate, the shape of time, the birth and death of stars, and the fragile beauty of the world held together by choices and stories, loss and love. When she removed her hand, the glow faded, and the hum softened, but something had changed. The forest, once closed to the world, had opened a door—not just to her, but to all those who would come after, seeking not to conquer but to understand. She turned away from the stone and walked back the way she came, the trees parting silently before her, the wind whispering her name in reverence. No record remains of her face or her words, but the forest remembers, as forests do, and sometimes, on the quietest days, if you listen closely beneath the rustling leaves and the breath of the earth, you can still hear her footsteps echoing faintly through the trees, a reminder that even the deepest mysteries are not beyond the reach of those who dare to ask.)]
